// This intentionally unresolved package demonstrates the deterministic ways
// in which surface `judge` claims guide later `...` synthesis. It contains one
// site in every synthesis grammar category: package declaration, type member,
// expression, statement list, and assembly instruction. Generated source may
// not add judgments, so every claim remains visible here in authored source.
// See specification sections 8-10.
package app

// Package judgments guide every synthesis site in the package. The attached
// contract therefore enters all four synthesis input hashes without being
// copied into generated source.
judge `Every generated fragment must preserve CONTRACT.md: Generated_Offset is
40, Generated_Record contains one i64 value, compute adds that value to the
offset, emit_barrier contains a DMB ISH instruction, and main checks that the
result is 42.`
    file "CONTRACT.md"

// This declaration site is resolved as an interface before procedure bodies
// that use Generated_Offset are completed. The direct prompt keeps the example
// about grammar boundaries and judgment guidance, not API invention.
... `Output exactly this Draft declaration:
Generated_Offset :: cast[i64](40)`

// Generated_Record demonstrates guidance local to a type member list. The
// claim is before the member site because only later sites in the same list
// receive a type-member judgment as synthesis context.
Generated_Record :: struct {
    judge `The following generated member must be exactly one field named value
of type i64 and must not add ownership or hidden state.`

    ... `Output exactly this Draft struct member:
value: i64,`
}

// compute demonstrates procedure-body judgment dominance. Every path to the
// expression site passes through the claim, so the resolver supplies it to the
// expression request together with record's checked type.
compute :: proc(record: Generated_Record) -> i64 {
    judge `The generated return expression must add record.value and
Generated_Offset exactly once each.`

    return ... `Output exactly this Draft expression:
record.value + Generated_Offset`
}

// emit_barrier demonstrates the parsed-assembly category. The judgment is an
// ordinary procedure statement that dominates the later asm expression's
// nested instruction site. The clobber declaration stays authored because it
// is part of the surrounding assembly boundary, while Codex supplies exactly
// one instruction row.
emit_barrier :: proc() {
    judge `The generated assembly instruction must be DMB ISH, and the
enclosing assembly block must declare its memory clobber.`

    asm aarch64 {
        clobber memory
        ... `Output exactly this Draft assembly instruction:
dmb ish`
    }
}

// main demonstrates the statement-list category. The preceding claim
// dominates the site and describes one assertion statement; it does not run
// while resolve generates that statement.
main :: proc() -> int {
    record := Generated_Record{value = 2}
    actual := compute(record)

    judge `The generated statement must assert that actual equals 42 and must
not change actual.`

    ... `Output exactly this Draft statement:
assert(actual == 42)`
    emit_barrier()
    return 0
}
